handoff:

There is no command. The hand idiom is mv, and the audit's note that nothing
checks where it lands is exact — measured 2026-09-18:

- A clean move works and check passes. needs: holds numbers and the registry
  keys on numbers, so dependencies and identity survive; only the hierarchy
  is positional.
- Moving an open ticket under a closed parent leaves "N: closed, but child M
  is open", which check reports and reopen repairs. Documented, and fine.
- Moving a closed parent back out of c/ silently reopens it and everything
  under it, and check is happy. State is location, so a stray mv resurrects
  closed work with nothing recording that it happened.

That third one is the argument for a command. Every other manual idiom in
the audit is wrong loudly — a bad field fails check, a bad line 3 stops
parsing — and this one is wrong quietly, in the direction of work looking
undone rather than done.

Against a command, and it deserves stating: mv is exactly right for the
clean case, the format is built so that moving a directory is the operation,
and a wrapper that only calls mv with a guard is thin. The question is
whether the guard is worth a verb.

If yes, the guard is the whole value: refuse a destination inside c/ unless
the ticket is being closed, refuse a move into the ticket's own subtree, and
say what the move does to the ticket's state before doing it.

Note for whoever takes this: tree renders by path, so a ticket under a closed
ancestor shows [x] while check calls it open. The two are not in conflict —
they answer different questions — but a reparent command that prints the
resulting state has to pick one and say which.

Done when the decision is written down, and if it is yes, the destination is
validated, a move that would change open/closed state says so, and the suite
covers the three cases above.


Done 2026-09-18. The answer to the ticket's question is yes, and the guard is
the whole value.

  pidly reparent 17 6      move it under 6
  pidly reparent -r 17     move it out to the top

The rule, and it is the only thing the command adds over mv: a reparent never
changes whether a ticket is closed. A closed ticket lands in the new parent's
c/ and stays closed; an open one lands directly under it.

Four refusals, each one a case mv would have done quietly:

- A closed destination. "4 is closed; moving 17 there would close it too —
  reopen 4 first, or close 17 deliberately."
- A parent inside the ticket's own subtree. mv refuses this too, but only
  after the caller has already believed it would work.
- A ticket that is already where it is being sent.
- An open ticket stranded inside a closed ancestor. That is check's finding
  and reopen's repair, and moving it would only hide it, so it says so and
  names the command.

On the ticket's note about tree and check disagreeing: nothing had to pick.
The stranded state is refused rather than reported on, so the command never
has to render a ticket whose two readings differ.

needs is untouched by a move and the registry keys on numbers, so nothing is
rewritten and there is no bookkeeping — that is what makes this a move rather
than a transaction. Tested explicitly.

The reference documents mv first and the command second, with the reason mv
is not enough, so the hand idiom stays the thing that works in a checkout
where pidly was never installed.

Covered in close.sh: the clean move, -r, a no-op move, self-parenting, needs
surviving, a closed ticket keeping its state, and all four refusals.
